VISUAL: Specifications for the Fosi Audio MD3 are displayed on screen, including DAC/Amp chips, input/output, sampling rate, and power.
AUDIO: 'bottom next to a 3.5 mm and then a 4.4 mm port. The rear, where the magnets are, has this nice faux leather texture and the face has this cute little screen where we can see our volume, navigate'

[01:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'menus, and then play with some other fun things like wallpapers and photos that you can upload. I can\'t say I was super into the customization. You can plug this into a computer and you\'ll see folders and you can add images and that\'s that\'s cute. You can have some'

[01:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fun with that, but I honestly didn\'t play with that much because I had a lot of headphones I wanted to try out. It\'s not a wireless DAC like some of the the Questyle or FiiO products that I\'ve been reviewing recently. This is more in line'

[01:46] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of the Fosi Audio IM4 earbuds, showing the branding.
AUDIO: 'with some of the other smaller DACs that I\'ve been playing with like the Onyx or the Shanling UA4. But, one of the best features that we can highlight right away are the dual USB ports which support pass-through charging. That\'s a'

[01:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nice touch. That\'s a really well-considered lifestyle design. Fosi also sent this along with their i M4 open ear IEM, which I\'ll also chat about a little in this video, too. Not like a full deep dive review, but some'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interesting little earbuds to pair with a portable DAC. I mostly listened in that combo that they sent, but I did a little testing with my Meze 105s, a couple options from Audeze, my Beyerdynamic DT 770s, and a couple fun'

[02:24] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: The MD3 is shown connected to a phone with both USB-C and 3.5mm cables.
AUDIO: 'options like my KZ Vader Pro. As the thing about testing a little portable DAC is getting a handle on how well it puts out a signal and whether it vibes with a pair of headphones or earbuds'

[02:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you already own. I was a bit concerned at first for how close the USB port is to the 3.5 mm because a lot of headphone and USB cables will have extra bulky shielding, and when that happens, those won\'t fit. But thankfully, the MD3'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can receive data from either port. Often when I was using this with my desktop PC, I would just plug in through the top port and leave the cables free. That way I didn\'t have any clearance issues with the cable shielding. I think there\'s a'

[03:01] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: The reviewer holds up the MD3 and IM4 earbuds, demonstrating their size.
AUDIO: 'limited expectation on little portable DACs, but it\'s an area that\'s improved radically over the last 5 years or so. The idea is to just plug in some low-power earbuds, but that there\'s enough power now to do a decent job of'

[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'driving larger cans. I\'ve tried to retrain my ears so I\'m not constantly jacking up the volume. So, with the i M4 earbuds, I rarely needed to turn these above 30 on the MD3 in the high amp'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'output mode to land a really comfortable listening volume. Cans like the Meze 105s are a little thirstier. I was comfortable there at around 45 on the dial. This is, of course, on a volume'

[03:39] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: The reviewer talks about the product.
AUDIO: 'scale of 0 to 100, by the way. It\'s nice having those granular individual 100 volume steps. The Audeze MM520 aren\'t particularly demanding, but needed a bit more juice around 50 to sound decently correct. I have plenty of headroom on a'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'large collection of earbuds and cans. I just don\'t think it\'s as likely folks will use something like this with a really high impedance thirsty headphone. But it put out decently with my aged DT 770 Pro 250 ohm Beyerdynamics that that'

[04:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I needed to turn it up to 75. This is not the best way to drive my old recording headphone, but it did work. I got some sound out of it. It sounded okay. The dangerous part of comparison'

[04:21] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: The MD3 is magnetically attached to the back of a phone, showcasing its primary feature.
AUDIO: 'testing was working up to some thirstier headphones and forgetting to turn the DAC back down again before returning to the IM04 which that was a a nasty little shock to my ears. Also, depending on'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your screen and timeout settings, your volume adjustments will stay screen off. Sticking it to the back of the phone, you can accidentally come in contact with the volume buttons, but not know the volume is sliding up or down on a'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'long press. I wish all adjustments would turn the screen back on. Just some visual identification that something has happened. Ditto that there could be some kind of pass-through audio control. I know we\'re not talking about true'

[04:58] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: The reviewer holds the MD3, discussing its features.
AUDIO: 'wireless, but I think a lot of consumers have gotten used to controlling audio through their earbuds or through something connected to their phone. Not just volume, but play pause, skipping tracks. There are buttons here that can'

[05:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'control fun little photo slideshows, but instead, I\'d like to be able to pause my music. Those are my two biggest gripes with the Magni DAC, but the mission here is well achieved. We want a high-quality'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'audio output that doesn\'t flop around in your pocket, and it shouldn\'t break the bank. Once you attach it to the back of a phone, it feels like a decently cohesive solution, and then you can pop the USB cable, port it over to a tablet,'

[05:37] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: The reviewer holds the IM4 earbuds, discussing their characteristics.
AUDIO: 'a laptop, or a desktop. I did a lot of listening connected to my desktop while writing the script for the review, and just, you know, kind of focusing for a second on the earbuds they sent, too. The IM4 earbuds. This was a fun'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'combination to test drive. This is a fun combo for this DAC. Premium priced, but not audiophile excessively expensive. We\'re not trying to break the bank. They\'re a nice punchy single driver IEM'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with good bass response. I think they arrive a little bright, but I love it when companies include ear tips and nozzles that help you tune them. So, even though I\'m not a skull rumbling basshead, I like the middle bassier ear'

[06:16] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: The reviewer holds the IM4 earbuds, emphasizing their sound.
AUDIO: 'tips. Just a little extra low-end bump, a little dash of flavor. I thought they sounded really good. Now, I don\'t know that I would recommend this total package, the IM4 and the MD3, as'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'someone\'s solo solution. Open-back earbuds are kind of interesting. I think a lot of people like the passive noise reduction they can get when an earbud is inserted into the ear canal. Casually, I'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wonder how many people there is concerned about having the airier sound and the wider sound stage that we get with the IM4 over wanting to use something like this in places where you can\'t count on the cleanest listening'

[06:53] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A set of "Bass" ear tips for the IM4 earbuds is shown.
AUDIO: 'environment. I\'m definitely biased because I got my start in IEMs in professional and live sound recording situations where I\'d need to block out the environment immediately around me and listen to the audio feed coming in'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through those IEMs. I really liked the IM4, though, and I immediately included them on a recent trek to the library. If you want an earbud and you\'re confident that you\'re going to listen often in areas where you can kind of control the'

[07:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'noise around you, it\'s a little quieter, this earbud gets that job done really well. I think it\'s always better to have a small collection of audio solutions for different situations. The IM4 could easily occupy a place on many people\'s'

[07:30] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of the MD3 connected to a phone, showing the USB-C and 3.5mm ports in use.
AUDIO: 'gear list. But, moving back to the DAC. I don\'t run a lot of headphones with a balanced connector these days. Mostly testing with my older Odyssey Euclid, and those are not particularly difficult'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to drive. More, I just like the options for folks who might prefer one cable standard over another. I think it\'s an important feature to include in this class of product as people grow and evolve, they might want a different headphone. It\'s nice to know for an'

[07:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inexpensive DAC that it can grow with them for a little while. And I also ran some basic quality tests, and I\'m happy to report that this era of mobile inexpensive audiophile is really good. I can hook this up to my mid-range audio'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recording interface and measure noise and distortion, and I\'m pretty sure we\'re getting to a point where the interface is contributing more to noise and distortion than the output of this'

[08:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'DAC. So, to that point, when we talk about quality, when when we get people to try this stuff, I think what impresses more people isn\'t some notion of high-res audio 32-bit 384'

[08:37] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of the MD3's audio output ports (3.5mm and 4.4mm).
AUDIO: 'Ks. Oh, just think all that data going to your brain. Especially as an on-ramp as people get started with this stuff, I don\'t know that it\'s always the DAC contributing more data to the audio'

[08:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stream, to the earbud or to the headphone that really gets people excited. I think it\'s the amp. Unfortunately, a lot of people have plugged nicer headphones and earbuds into mediocre laptop and computer ports, and then they think audio stuff is'

[09:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'underwhelming or or maybe that it\'s outright snake oil. I spent a lot on these headphones and my music sounds kind of the same. So, connecting to a little dongle DAC and hearing some decent headphones being properly driven, getting more color and space and tone'

[09:18] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: The reviewer talks about the product.
AUDIO: 'and articulation, it\'s not just louder, but providing more depth and character, really driving those drivers. I think that\'s where we\'re more likely to impress new consumers curious about more premium audio, and that these'

[09:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'experiences can arrive as inexpensively as they do with some lifestyle considerations for how it might pair with a phone, and that\'s a really nice touch. And then there\'s just some cute stuff to play with, too. The tricky'

[09:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thing for Fosi Audio is how good the competition has gotten around $100. Now, my go-to solutions recently have been the Shanling UA4 and the Onyx Alpha, and these are all pretty close in price. The Shanling and'

[09:59] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: The Onyx Alpha DAC is shown connected to a phone, for comparison.
AUDIO: 'the Onyx are smaller, but don\'t have the magnets, so they\'re going to flop around when they\'re connected to your phone. May- maybe that puts more strain on a phone\'s USB port if that\'s all in your pocket and getting kind of mashed'

[10:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around. The Shanling and the Onyx have a little more customizing they can do with tuning and EQ presets. I like how both of those DACs can control music playback, not just play pause, but skipping tracks if you go in and adjust'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your menu settings. And both of those DACs are going to put out a little bit more power, so you can drive even thirstier headphones than what you can drive on the Fosi. But, the Fosi has the nicer screen for seeing your volume and navigating your menus. The Fosi has the'

[10:36] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: The reviewer holds up the Shanling UA4 and Onyx Alpha DACs for comparison.
AUDIO: 'magnets, you can stick it to the back of your phone, and the Fosi has that power pass-through charging. I think this is the better lifestyle companion, even if those other smaller DACs are literally better audiophile solutions. Considering'

[10:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the headphones you\'re going to pair them with, I think all three sound really good, at least with all of the headphones that I tried them with, but the Shanling and the Onyx feel a little more audiophile focused, while the Fosi feels a little more fun and trying to'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
